<?xml version="1.0"?>
<info xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
	  xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://apps.nextcloud.com/schema/apps/info.xsd">
	<id>versioniq</id>
	<name>Versioniq</name>
	<summary>Install any earlier or newer version of already installed Nextcloud apps</summary>
	<description>> ⚠️ **Active development — not for production use yet.** This app is under active development. Although it may carry a stable release status, **please do not use it in production environments before 12 June 2026.** See [conduction.nl/apps](https://conduction.nl/apps) for release planning and what this app does.

Versioniq gives Nextcloud administrators the ability to roll back apps to previous versions or install specific newer versions. Essential for debugging, testing compatibility, and recovering from broken updates.</description>
	<version>1.5.2</version>
	<licence>EUPL-1.2</licence>
	<author mail="info@conduction.nl" homepage="https://conduction.nl">Conduction B.V.</author>
	<namespace>Versioniq</namespace>
	<category>tools</category>
	<bugs>https://github.com/ConductionNL/versioniq/issues</bugs>
	<repository>https://github.com/ConductionNL/versioniq</repository>
	<dependencies>
		<!-- Floor raised 31 -> 32 with the fleet. composer.json requires php ^8.3
		     and nextcloud/ocp dev-master, so this app has never been analysed
		     against the NC 31 API surface — the 31 claim was advertised and
		     backed by nothing. Every core Conduction app declares 32-34. -->
		<nextcloud min-version="32" max-version="34"/>
	</dependencies>
	<background-jobs>
		<job>OCA\Versioniq\BackgroundJob\AdvisoryRefreshJob</job>
		<job>OCA\Versioniq\BackgroundJob\PatExpiryWarningJob</job>
		<job>OCA\Versioniq\BackgroundJob\AutoUpdateJob</job>
		<job>OCA\Versioniq\BackgroundJob\PruneAuditJob</job>
		<job>OCA\Versioniq\BackgroundJob\PinReconcileJob</job>
	</background-jobs>
	<repair-steps>
		<!-- THE app_versions -> versioniq RENAME IS A DATA MIGRATION.
		     Nextcloud has no in-place app-id upgrade: the new id is simply a
		     different app, so every store keyed by app id (oc_appconfig,
		     oc_preferences) becomes unreachable the moment <id> changes. The
		     two Migrate* steps carry those rows across.

		     They are registered under BOTH <install> and <post-migration>, and
		     FIRST in each. <install> is the path a real deployment takes,
		     because Nextcloud discovers `versioniq` as a brand-new app rather
		     than as an upgrade of `app_versions`; <post-migration> covers a
		     dev box whose app directory was renamed in place.

		     FIRST in the list matters: anything that writes fresh defaults
		     must run after the copy, or the never-overwrite guard in the copy
		     sees the fresh value and silently discards the admin's stored one.

		     NOT MIGRATED, deliberately:
		       - the `app_versions_pats` and `app_versions_audit` TABLES keep
		         their old prefix — see the comment on their TABLE_NAME
		         constants in lib/Db/. Table names are not keyed by app id, so
		         the rename does not touch them and renaming them by hand would
		         be a destructive migration for no gain.
		       - oc_activity / oc_notifications rows written under the old app
		         id: Nextcloud owns those tables and offers no supported way to
		         re-key them. Historic entries simply stop being listed. -->
		<post-migration>
			<step>OCA\Versioniq\Repair\MigrateAppConfigKeys</step>
			<step>OCA\Versioniq\Repair\MigrateUserPreferences</step>
			<!--
				THE THIRD STORE KEYED BY APP ID, AND THE TWO ABOVE DO NOT COVER
				IT. `oc_appconfig` and `oc_preferences` are Nextcloud's; this one
				is OpenRegister's `openregister_schemas.application`.

				OpenRegister resolves the two halves of a configuration by
				DIFFERENT keys: a REGISTER by slug alone, a SCHEMA by the PAIR
				(application, slug) via findByApplicationAndSlug(). An import
				passes `appId: Application::APP_ID` = `versioniq`, while any
				schema written under the old id still carries
				`application = app_versions` — so the pair matches nothing, and
				ImportHandler's not-found branch is not an error path, it is the
				"create a new one" path. The import then builds a SECOND, EMPTY
				schema set while every stored object stays bound to the old rows.
				Nothing errors; the collections just render empty.

				`app_versions`, not `app-versions`: the hyphen is the GitHub
				repository name, the underscore is the app id this column holds.

				This app keeps its own data in the `app_versions_*` tables rather
				than in OpenRegister, so on a clean instance the step finds
				nothing and says "nothing to do". It is registered as the guard
				for an instance that DOES carry such rows, where the failure is
				silent rather than loud.

				Refuses rather than merges where a slug already has a twin under
				the new application id, never deletes a schema, and never throws
				— an escaping exception in <install> aborts the install.
			-->
			<step>OCA\Versioniq\Repair\MigrateSchemaApplicationId</step>
			<!--
				The background jobs moved from OCA\Versioniq\Cron to
				OCA\Versioniq\BackgroundJob (ADR-100 Decision 3), and the <job>
				entries below are a REGISTRATION instruction rather than a
				description of state: on upgrade Nextcloud ADDS a job it does
				not have, but never removes one whose class disappeared —
				it cannot tell a renamed class from one that is merely
				unavailable this boot.

				So without this step the instance holds BOTH rows, and the dead
				one cannot be instantiated on any cron tick. Measured on a live
				instance during this move: oc_jobs carried
				OCA\OpenCatalogi\Cron\DirectorySync alongside its BackgroundJob
				replacement. Idempotent — a fresh install removes nothing.
			-->
			<step>OCA\Versioniq\Repair\RemoveRetiredCronJobs</step>
		</post-migration>
		<install>
			<step>OCA\Versioniq\Repair\MigrateAppConfigKeys</step>
			<step>OCA\Versioniq\Repair\MigrateUserPreferences</step>
			<!-- Schema half of the same rename; see the <post-migration> block.
			     An app-id rename presents to Nextcloud as a FRESH INSTALL, so
			     this block is the one that actually fires on the upgrade the
			     step exists for. -->
			<step>OCA\Versioniq\Repair\MigrateSchemaApplicationId</step>
		</install>
	</repair-steps>
	<commands>
		<command>OCA\Versioniq\Command\ListVersions</command>
		<command>OCA\Versioniq\Command\InstallVersion</command>
	</commands>
	<settings>
		<admin>OCA\Versioniq\Settings\Admin</admin>
		<admin-section>OCA\Versioniq\Sections\AdminSection</admin-section>
	</settings>
</info>
